>-4.50 [RR2.7 .307/.114]. SHAPE: on 3/8 (ETH/LINK/SOL) the val-grid itself selects c*=hold; where it commits to a real R:R, OOS is ASYMMETRIC -- the rare R:R 'win' is marginal and still deep-negative (BNB +1.1, XRP +0.3) while R:R LOSSES are large (DOGE -9.2, LTC -7.0), the wide-SL c* carrying a fat left tail that val-selection underestimates. So per-symbol-tuned B does NOT lift R:R above hold-60s OOS -- consistent with the (incomplete) reused-HP baseline grid (BNB/BTC/DOGE c*=hold or c* < hold on test) and the walkforward row. Binding constraint stays adverse selection on PASSIVE MAKER fills; next lever = EXECUTION (taker entry per RESEARCH_LOG s14 +5.6bp; realistic resting-maker exit), not R:R/HP tuning. METHOD: the new grid models entry-MISS + adverse SL-knockout absent in the s14 GRU grid, legitimately inverting the old big-TP/small-SL preference toward wide-SL (c* SL pins near the 0.40 grid ceiling, e.g. BNB 0.391); residual optimism = touch-modeled TP exit (tight TP filled too easily) -> neither grid's argmax R:R is ground truth, robust read is OOS = hold. Optuna is not at fault: it maximizes inner-val AUC as designed; its window-overfit propagates into downstream c* selection (DOGE val RR1.9 edge +0.26bp -> test -9.2bp) -- expected optimizer behavior, not a defect.", "params": {"b_trials": 25, "budget_per_day": 10, "exit_model": "touch (resting-maker exit NOT modeled)", "gate": "apred", "gate_pct": 5.0, "n_configs": 94966, "n_sl": 317, "n_tp": 317, "nf_rate": 0.05, "optuna_b": true, "queue_mult_entry": 0, "rr_clip": [0.5, 30.0], "seed": 0, "sl_grid_pct": [0.03, 0.4], "tp_grid_pct": [0.05, 1.5]}, "repro_cmd": "python3 subs60_xgb_b2_grid.py --symbols BNB BTC DOGE ETH LINK LTC SOL XRP --n-tp 317 --n-sl 317 --budget 10 --optuna-b --b-trials 25", "setup": "Per-symbol R:R (TP/SL) CONFIG-AXIS map vs hold-60s, with per-symbol Optuna-tuned direction head B. Per symbol: train A vol-gate + Stage-1 B (hold), select c* = argmax VAL daily-budget(10/day) net maker-EV over a